  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Effective  strategies and  approaches on formalization  in Asia and the Pacific</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Asia-Pacific region has a high incidence of informality. Around 65.9% of total employment remains informal, representing approximately 1.3 billion workers. Despite various policies and interventions aimed at advancing formalization,  yet most countries have not fully adopted comprehensive strategies and  integrated approaches to  formalization. Enhancing the capacity of countries to develop, implement, and monitor integrated approaches to formalization is critical, encompassing aspects such as formalization of employment, economic units, activities, access to social security, and extension of labor protection to all. 
The ILO has provided technical assistance through generating evidence and research on the informal economy, capacity building of tripartite constituents, and promoting the documentation and dissemination of good practices.
For the current biennium, the ILO&apos;s strategy focuses on improving the capacity of tripartite constituents to produce, understand, and apply findings of diagnostics of informality at national and sectoral levels. This involves enhancing their ability to develop, adopt, operationalize, and monitor integrated approaches and strategies for formalization. The ILO aims to achieve these goals by generating evidence and research on the status, incidence, forms, and drivers of the informal economy in the region. Additionally, the strategy includes capacity building on formalization strategies, tools, and approaches, promoting the documentation and dissemination of good practices, tools, strategies, and coordination arrangements that facilitate transitions toward formalization, including through south-south exchanges. Producing tools and knowledge products to facilitate transitions toward formalization is also a key component of the strategy.
The primary beneficiaries of the ILO&apos;s intervention are workers in the informal economy who stand to gain from improved formalization efforts. Key stakeholders include national governments, employe</narrative>
  </description>
